maybe, my laptop, my machine.

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:25.090
And it's talking to
the Sheets service

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:27.590
to make modifications
on my behalf.

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:29.560
So we start by
requiring the package.

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:35.550
Then we initialize
the Sheets client.

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:39.040
And then, right above
there, we see a long string.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:41.330
That is the authorization scope.

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:43.900
So the scope is
the level of access

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:46.810
we want to grant to our script.

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:48.970
I don't know about
you, but when I'm

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:51.400
installing a new application,
I get really nervous when

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:55.450
it starts to ask for a lot of
permission to my user data,

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:58.090
if it wants full
access to my Drive,

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:01.030
it wants to read my emails,
it wants my location history,

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:05.560
shoe size, mother's maiden
name, social security number.

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:10.000
It is really bad practice to
ask for more access to user data

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:11.900
than you actually need.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:15.750
And we'll be talking about best
practices for Auth later today.

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:17.500
And then also, don't
worry about trying

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:19.690
to memorize the code that
we're going to show you.

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:22.780
All of it is on GitHub, and
then we will send out this link

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:23.530
after the talk.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:27.980
So back to "Hello World!"

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:31.580
We make our call to
actually write the data,

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:36.810
then we need to tell the service
where to insert "Hello World!"

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:41.390
So, in Sheets, we specify cells
by what we call A1 notation.

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:45.290
So today, we're actually just
writing to the very first cell.

00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:49.040
We're using modern syntax.

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:51.740
And, once we run that--
and if we go to the browser

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:53.630
and actually open the Sheet--

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:55.200
this is what it looks like.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:57.270
We wrote to that
very first cell.

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:01.130
And if you remember from that
API call that we just showed,

00:06:01.130 --> 00:06:03.320
you need to specify
the spreadsheet ID.

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:06.860
Well, that ID, conveniently,
is the same one

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:09.800
you find in the URL if you're
looking in the browser.

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:18.260
So if you're at the point that
you are using the Sheets API,

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:21.460
you're probably going to need
to write to more than just

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:23.200
one cell at a time.

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:26.680
And the best way to do
this is to actually group

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:30.280
your write requests
together when it's possible.

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:32.230
We have this concept
called the batch update--

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:33.370
the batch update method--

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:36.970
in many G Suite APIs,
and it's the best way

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:39.830
to make modifications in bulk.

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:42.070
So why should you group
your requests together

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:44.030
with batching?

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:46.410
There are two main reasons.

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:49.370
So the first is, every
time any application

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:52.610
makes a network call, there's
a little bit of overhead,

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:55.640
some latency for setting all
that up and receiving it.

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:57.710
When you group
them together, you

00:06:57.710 --> 00:07:02.160
can actually reduce that total
latency for all of those calls.

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:05.900
The second is, these
APIs are free to use.

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:08.600
However, you are bound
by quota restrictions.

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:11.870
So when you group them
together into a single request,

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:15.760
you'll be using
dramatically less quota.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:17.520
So each one of those
tasks that you see--

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:19.770
that's going to actually be
a different type of write,

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:21.490
and you can still
group them together.

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:25.090
So let's look at all the
different things you can do.

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:27.910
Think of batch update as
like a big fancy list,

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:31.060
or like a bucket for all
your different modifications.

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:34.030
It's like a really big
hammer for many of the tasks

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:35.270
that you can do in the UI.

00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:38.320
So we already saw that
you can write data.

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:41.237
We'll be talking a lot
about adding charts today.

00:07:41.237 --> 00:07:42.820
And you can do other
things, like even

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:44.200
add developer metadata.

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:49.190
So now that we have
a basic toolkit

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:51.380
for working with
the Sheets service,

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:54.270
let's actually try to
solve a real-world problem.

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:57.080
So I sit next to
Franzie, and I frequently

00:07:57.080 --> 00:08:02.520
see her looking at the Insights
tab for the Node Core repo.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:08.480
So this is a repo that
has the Node.js runtime.

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:12.170
She is very concerned with
the health of this repo.

00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:15.890
The inside tab gives
you a lot of metrics

00:08:15.890 --> 00:08:18.890
about the number of issues
and pull requests over time.

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:24.630
However, this tab only
gives you insights

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:27.720
into what's going
on within GitHub.

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:30.030
But if you've ever had
a long day at work,

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:33.030
you know that there are
many different things that

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:36.170
go into how productive we are.

00:08:36.170 --> 00:08:39.059
You can't really
see things like, oh,

00:08:39.059 --> 00:08:41.610
are people being blocked
by the Jenkins CI,

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:43.650
and that's reducing the
number of pull requests

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:45.300
they could be making?

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:48.480
You can't see if maybe
they're stuck in traffic.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:52.060
That's why they
can't close issues.

00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:55.080
There's a lot of
things happening.

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:58.500
What if just how well-fed
people are dramatically

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:00.580
affects their productivity?

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:02.910
Franzie wants to get
a holistic picture

00:09:02.910 --> 00:09:05.070
into what helps people
be more productive.

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:10.020
So let's focus in on
one of those questions

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:11.850
that we're very curious
about but that's

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:13.860
kind of hard to define.

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:17.560
We're going to go a little
silly today, so bear with us.

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:19.260
We want to experiment
and see if we

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:23.265
can see if food satisfaction
yields better GitHub metrics.

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:30.660
So this is a hard problem, not
from a technical perspective,

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:32.670
but a real-world perspective.

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:36.330
We have our contributors at
their machines, we have GitHub,

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:37.910
and we have the food.

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:41.930
We want an application to tie it
all together so we can automate

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:44.510
gathering these
metrics, which is

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:47.570
why we're going to use
Sheets, so we can quickly

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:50.150
manipulate that data.

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:52.070
We're going to write
some code in Node

00:09:52.070 --> 00:09:53.910
to monitor the Node repo.

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:56.573
So let's get started
by breaking this down.

00:09:56.573 --> 00:09:57.990
FRANZISKA HINKELMANN:
So my theory

00:09:57.990 --> 00:10:01.040
is that we work better
if we're well-fed.

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:03.620
But we want to see the metrics.

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:06.320
Let's look at the
data to prove this.

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:09.290
First thing we have to do is
collect the data as a new set.

00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:11.880
We're going to get this
from the GitHub repository.

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:15.350
We are specifically interested
in how many pull requests are

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:17.300
merged, how many
issues are closed,

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:19.760
and overall, how many
open issues and pull

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:22.080
requests do we have?

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:24.080
So how do we get this data?

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:26.360
Well, GitHub has a
very nice REST API,

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:30.170
and if we use Octokit to GitHub
REST API client for JavaScript,

00:10:30.170 --> 00:10:32.360
it's very easy to get this data.

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:34.670
And the API gives us
exactly the same data

00:10:34.670 --> 00:10:36.590
that we would get
in the Insights tab.

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:39.380
So it's really just a
few very straightforward

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:42.140
API calls to get this
data programmatically.

00:10:42.140 --> 00:10:44.120
And, once we have
the data from GitHub,

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:46.610
we will write it
to a spreadsheet.

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:48.663
So, just like in the
"Hello World!" example

00:10:48.663 --> 00:10:50.330
where we wrote "Hello
World!", now we're

00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:53.670
writing the numbers
that we got from GitHub.

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:57.450
Of course, I could have console
logged the results from GitHub,

00:10:57.450 --> 00:11:01.110
copied that, and then manually
pasted it into a spreadsheet.

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:02.160
But that's manual.

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:03.730
I might be making mistakes.

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:08.240
I don't want to do it,
so I'm using an API call.

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:10.790
Now onto the food part.

00:11:10.790 --> 00:11:14.550
We love metrics and
surveys, so we did a survey.

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:16.370
In this survey, we
figure out how happy

00:11:16.370 --> 00:11:19.520
the contributors are with
their food on a daily basis.

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:20.690
Super scientific.

00:11:23.470 --> 00:11:25.090
Every time you
use a Google form,

00:11:25.090 --> 00:11:28.090
the results are automatically
written to a spreadsheet,

00:11:28.090 --> 00:11:29.650
so here we just
configure the form

00:11:29.650 --> 00:11:31.450
to use the same
spreadsheet that we also

00:11:31.450 --> 00:11:36.310
used for our write calls
of the GitHub data.

00:11:36.310 --> 00:11:36.830
All right.

00:11:36.830 --> 00:11:40.270
Now that we have the data both
for GitHub and for food, now

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:43.610
we are interested in whether
there are any correlations.

00:11:43.610 --> 00:11:45.980
If we are hungry, do
we close more issues

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:48.350
because we want to get
done and leave to get food?

00:11:48.350 --> 00:11:51.170
Or if we had too much
food, are we much slower

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:52.910
and we don't merge
any pull requests?

00:11:52.910 --> 00:11:57.780
Or if we're hungry, maybe we
just open issue after issue.

00:11:57.780 --> 00:11:59.450
How can we see
these correlations?

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:02.990
Well, a quick way is with
a chart, so let's do that.

00:12:02.990 --> 00:12:04.040
Here's the chart.

00:12:04.040 --> 00:12:05.910
We'll look at the results later.

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:09.180
For now, we're just interested
in how we get this chart.

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:11.400
Of course, you can go to
the Sheet you've highlighted

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:13.130
and then click Add Chart.

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:15.480
But we don't want
to do this manually,

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:17.270
so we would use an API call.

00:12:17.270 --> 00:12:20.370
Let's see what the code
for this looks like.

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:22.940
So if we want to
create a chart, we

00:12:22.940 --> 00:12:27.790
have to create an
Add Chart request.

00:12:27.790 --> 00:12:29.950
This Add Chart
request has a chart,

00:12:29.950 --> 00:12:33.070
and we have to pass on the
specification for the chart.

00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:35.200
Once we've built our
request, then we're

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:39.636
using our friend batchUpdate
to send this Add Chart request.

00:12:39.636 --> 00:12:42.860
A few chart parameters I
want to point out here--

00:12:42.860 --> 00:12:45.050
one thing you have to
specify is the type.

00:12:45.050 --> 00:12:46.840
So we are creating a line chart.

00:12:46.840 --> 00:12:48.550
Then you need the domain.

00:12:48.550 --> 00:12:51.080
The domain is your x-axis.

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:52.390
So in our case, it's the date.

00:12:52.390 --> 00:12:55.230
We want to see how are the
trends changing over time,

00:12:55.230 --> 00:12:57.700
so we put the date
as the domain.

00:12:57.700 --> 00:13:00.520
And then, probably the most
important part of a chart

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:02.730
is the series, the data series.

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:05.380
So, in our case, the numbers
we got from GitHub of how many

00:13:05.380 --> 00:13:08.020
issues and pull requests.

00:13:08.020 --> 00:13:10.490
So our architecture
overall looks like this.

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:12.130
We have two data sources.

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:14.980
We pull the data and we
write it to a spreadsheet,

00:13:14.980 --> 00:13:17.350
and then the sheet
creates a chart for us.

00:13:17.350 --> 00:13:19.570
And we're doing all
of this locally.

00:13:19.570 --> 00:13:21.400
We're not opening
a sheet and running

00:13:21.400 --> 00:13:22.630
anything inside the sheet.

00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:25.965
All this Node code
runs on our machine.

00:13:25.965 --> 00:13:28.090
We can control the Node
version that we're running,

00:13:28.090 --> 00:13:30.610
and it's just interacting
with the REST API.

00:13:30.610 --> 00:13:33.205
Anu is now going to take
this one step further.

00:13:33.205 --> 00:13:34.270
ANU SRIVASTAVA: Whoo-hoo.

00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:36.780
Yeah.

00:13:36.780 --> 00:13:40.260
So we're gathering the data,
and we're getting insights.

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:42.900
However, it's only running
locally on a machine,

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:45.930
so only one team member
has access to it.

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:47.910
We should really host
this as a service.

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:50.720
We are in the Cloud
track, after all.

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:53.630
So ideally, this would
be a fully-fledged app

00:13:53.630 --> 00:13:55.310
with a client and a server.

00:13:55.310 --> 00:13:57.770
But we actually know
that it's really not

00:13:57.770 --> 00:14:00.295
just those two parts.

00:14:00.295 --> 00:14:02.670
If you want to get all those
good principles that we have

00:14:02.670 --> 00:14:06.320
in Cloud, like high
availability, fault tolerance,

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:08.690
we probably need to
add multiple servers.

00:14:08.690 --> 00:14:10.760
When you have multiple
servers, you probably

00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:15.140
need a load balancer to
distribute those requests.

00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:17.480
Maybe you need a
cache somewhere.

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:19.190
There's a lot of
different pieces

00:14:19.190 --> 00:14:23.510
that you need to set up
to have a real server.

00:14:23.510 --> 00:14:26.600
Franzie and I only want
to focus on our code, not

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:28.440
the infrastructure.

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:31.070
So we decide to go
investigate other options.

00:14:33.730 --> 00:14:37.030
This is how we land
on serverless in GCP.

00:14:37.030 --> 00:14:40.120
So with serverless, you just
get to upload your code,

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:42.220
and then the Cloud
provider figures out

00:14:42.220 --> 00:14:44.960
how to place and scale
it on your behalf.

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:47.530
We don't need to know, or
even really want to know,

00:14:47.530 --> 00:14:50.800
anything about the machines
that our code is running on.

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:54.700
And you only have to pay for the
time that your code is running.

00:14:54.700 --> 00:14:58.548
So this is really great, because
to do a fetch of all the data

00:14:58.548 --> 00:15:01.090
and produce a chart, it doesn't
take more than a few minutes.

00:15:01.090 --> 00:15:03.700
But if we were to
put this on a VM,

00:15:03.700 --> 00:15:08.920
we'd probably be paying for that
to just sit there idly 24/7.

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:10.600
Then "Open" over
there just means

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:13.150
that you get to use the
tools and the frameworks

00:15:13.150 --> 00:15:16.030
and the languages that
you're already familiar with.

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:18.010
Since we're working
with Node today,

00:15:18.010 --> 00:15:20.230
we're going to be using
the Express framework.

00:15:20.230 --> 00:15:21.870
If you're working
with Python, you'll

00:15:21.870 --> 00:15:24.280
be using Flask, and all
of those good pairs.

00:15:28.270 --> 00:15:30.100
So there's a few
different products

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:31.900
in the serverless family.

00:15:31.900 --> 00:15:34.090
We decide to go with
Cloud Functions.

00:15:34.090 --> 00:15:36.280
So Cloud Functions,
in a nutshell--

00:15:36.280 --> 00:15:41.020
they are narrow, focused bits of
code that you upload to Google,

00:15:41.020 --> 00:15:43.810
and then you trigger
them by events.

00:15:43.810 --> 00:15:49.120
So we have background events
that they respond to--

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:50.678
some of your other
Cloud services,

00:15:50.678 --> 00:15:52.720
you can think of them as
like glue for connecting

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:54.370
all these pieces.

00:15:54.370 --> 00:15:57.970
Maybe you trigger an event
by uploading some files

00:15:57.970 --> 00:16:00.010
to your GCS bucket.

00:16:00.010 --> 00:16:03.010
Maybe you're listening to
incoming Pub/Sub messages.

00:16:03.010 --> 00:16:06.400
We're actually going to be
working with HTTP functions

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:07.570
today.

00:16:07.570 --> 00:16:09.970
Those are more common,
and they are triggered

00:16:09.970 --> 00:16:12.190
by what you'd probably expect--

00:16:12.190 --> 00:16:14.440
HTTP requests.

00:16:14.440 --> 00:16:16.570
They're really good
for things where

00:16:16.570 --> 00:16:18.580
you need to either
process data, like us,

00:16:18.580 --> 00:16:21.250
or maybe you're calling
external services.

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:23.530
So, in the real world,
people use them for things

00:16:23.530 --> 00:16:26.010
like, OK, I want to do
credit card payments,

00:16:26.010 --> 00:16:28.240
so maybe I'm talking to Stripe.

00:16:28.240 --> 00:16:29.780
Maybe I want to
send text messages,

00:16:29.780 --> 00:16:32.950
so I'll be using Twilio.

00:16:32.950 --> 00:16:37.498
Let's look at a quick "Hello
World!" for HTTP functions.

00:16:37.498 --> 00:16:39.040
So I keep saying
the word "function."

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:40.610
Is that really what I mean?

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:41.110
Yes.

00:16:41.110 --> 00:16:45.220
With two lines of code, you have
a fully operational back end.

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:47.890
After you deploy
this, you don't need

00:16:47.890 --> 00:16:51.040
any additional configuration,
like a gateway.

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:55.360
With Google Cloud Functions,
you get a fully-provisioned end

00:16:55.360 --> 00:16:57.790
point on a
fully-qualified domain.

00:16:57.790 --> 00:17:01.820
And this handles the
SSL [INAUDIBLE] for you.

00:17:01.820 --> 00:17:03.220
And we mentioned the framework.

00:17:03.220 --> 00:17:05.540
So those requests and
response objects--

00:17:05.540 --> 00:17:07.270
those are from the
Express framework.

00:17:10.150 --> 00:17:13.398
So, now that we know a little
bit about Cloud functions,

00:17:13.398 --> 00:17:14.940
we're going to keep
that code that we

00:17:14.940 --> 00:17:16.319
had in our first version.

00:17:16.319 --> 00:17:19.500
We're just going to wrap
it up in our local script,

00:17:19.500 --> 00:17:22.079
and then deploy it so we
have a serverless back end

00:17:22.079 --> 00:17:23.579
for our system.

00:17:23.579 --> 00:17:27.270
This service will read our
data from our external sources,

00:17:27.270 --> 00:17:29.880
and then produce a chart,
all while running in a data

00:17:29.880 --> 00:17:31.950
center somewhere in the cloud.

00:17:35.010 --> 00:17:36.930
So we upload the
code that we already

00:17:36.930 --> 00:17:39.450
have together with
an exported function.

00:17:39.450 --> 00:17:40.890
We'll call it githubChart.

00:17:40.890 --> 00:17:43.570
And then that calls
our main logic.

00:17:43.570 --> 00:17:46.300
So after all that
processing is done,

00:17:46.300 --> 00:17:49.860
we send a 200 status code,
just like a "Hello World!"

00:17:49.860 --> 00:17:51.960
And then here, we're
going to send back

00:17:51.960 --> 00:17:54.360
the ID of the spreadsheet,
which has our chart.

00:17:58.100 --> 00:17:59.620
So how do we deploy
this function?

00:17:59.620 --> 00:18:02.200
That sounds like it
might be hard or scary.

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:03.440
It's actually really easy.

00:18:03.440 --> 00:18:05.710
You can use the gcloud CLI.

00:18:05.710 --> 00:18:08.530
The gcloud CLI is a
tool that you can use

00:18:08.530 --> 00:18:10.570
to manage all your GCP tasks.

00:18:10.570 --> 00:18:13.090
So with functions,
you just have to pass

00:18:13.090 --> 00:18:15.620
in the name of your
exported function.

00:18:15.620 --> 00:18:17.290
So that's why we see
githubChart again.

00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:22.980
So it uploads your code.

00:18:22.980 --> 00:18:25.530
It picks up your
package.json, not

00:18:25.530 --> 00:18:27.640
the node modules themselves.

00:18:27.640 --> 00:18:30.810
So this is really nice if you
have slow data connections.

00:18:30.810 --> 00:18:32.430
It's just picking
up your source code,

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:35.170
and then it built it
in the cloud for you.

00:18:35.170 --> 00:18:37.320
We also edit and
deploy the function

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:39.660
straight from the
Cloud console as well.

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:41.790
The Cloud console
is the web interface

00:18:41.790 --> 00:18:43.960
where you manage
your Cloud services,

00:18:43.960 --> 00:18:47.130
and then you turn on
APIs and look at all

00:18:47.130 --> 00:18:48.880
your monitoring and
stuff like that.

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:55.760
So the code is in the
cloud, but it still

00:18:55.760 --> 00:18:58.880
needs to be triggered
manually by a human.

00:18:58.880 --> 00:19:01.280
We want to keep pulling
in this data regularly

00:19:01.280 --> 00:19:03.970
and keep tracking our
productivity over time.

00:19:06.930 --> 00:19:09.470
So instead of needing
a person to trigger

00:19:09.470 --> 00:19:13.610
this function every day, we can
actually schedule the function

00:19:13.610 --> 00:19:15.980
by using Cloud Scheduler.

00:19:15.980 --> 00:19:18.560
In my opinion, Cloud
Scheduler is just

00:19:18.560 --> 00:19:20.390
big, fancy cron in the cloud.

00:19:23.110 --> 00:19:26.040
We set up this cron job in
the Cloud console as well.

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:29.920
You use the same syntax as
if you were using basic cron.

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:34.960
We have ours set to run
every morning at 9:00 AM.

00:19:34.960 --> 00:19:37.480
And then, down at the
bottom, we specify

00:19:37.480 --> 00:19:40.810
that HTTP URL we got back
from Cloud Functions.

00:19:40.810 --> 00:19:45.610
So now we have a back end job to
do all of our data collection.

00:19:45.610 --> 00:19:49.710
And Franzie's going to take
you through adding a client.

00:19:49.710 --> 00:19:52.210
FRANZISKA HINKELMANN: So when
we trigger and HTTP end point,

00:19:52.210 --> 00:19:54.110
our code is run on a function.

00:19:54.110 --> 00:19:56.590
But for more functionality,
it would be nice

00:19:56.590 --> 00:19:59.320
if we had a front end client
so we don't have to memorize

00:19:59.320 --> 00:20:02.470
a bunch of HTTP end points.

00:20:02.470 --> 00:20:05.290
So we add a client
that sends out emails

00:20:05.290 --> 00:20:07.240
to the team with the new chart.

00:20:07.240 --> 00:20:08.740
And since we're
dealing with emails,

00:20:08.740 --> 00:20:11.530
we're going to use the
Gmail API for this task.

00:20:11.530 --> 00:20:13.600
Let's look at our front
end code for this.

00:20:13.600 --> 00:20:15.670
So just like in the
back end with Node.js,

00:20:15.670 --> 00:20:17.890
we have client libraries
on the front end.

00:20:17.890 --> 00:20:20.410
And then for Gmail, if
you want to send an email,

00:20:20.410 --> 00:20:25.030
it's really straightforward,
Gmail.users.messages.send.

00:20:25.030 --> 00:20:27.310
You have to include
your raw email here.

00:20:27.310 --> 00:20:30.880
That's just Base64 encoded
off your content, which

00:20:30.880 --> 00:20:34.650
could be plain text or HTML.

00:20:34.650 --> 00:20:36.960
So now we have the
front end OR cron

00:20:36.960 --> 00:20:39.060
job triggering the
Cloud function,

00:20:39.060 --> 00:20:41.402
which pulls the new data
and creates a chart.

00:20:41.402 --> 00:20:43.110
And when that is done,
then the front end

00:20:43.110 --> 00:20:45.030
will send out an email
with this new chart

00:20:45.030 --> 00:20:48.150
to everybody on the team.

00:20:48.150 --> 00:20:50.840
Is that the whole story, though?

00:20:50.840 --> 00:20:51.620
Just kidding.

00:20:51.620 --> 00:20:54.250
We laughed that off.

00:20:54.250 --> 00:20:57.190
Authorization-- you don't
want others to send out

00:20:57.190 --> 00:20:59.950
emails on your behalf,
and you don't want anybody

00:20:59.950 --> 00:21:02.090
to read and write
your spreadsheets.

00:21:02.090 --> 00:21:04.390
So let's have a look at
how we do authorization

00:21:04.390 --> 00:21:06.750
in our application.

00:21:06.750 --> 00:21:09.887
In our front end, we have
an Authorize or Login button

00:21:09.887 --> 00:21:11.470
that triggers the
Google Sign-in flow.

00:21:15.290 --> 00:21:19.770
Google Sigin-in is a secure
authentication system

00:21:19.770 --> 00:21:22.830
that allows users to log in
with their Google account

00:21:22.830 --> 00:21:26.310
that they already have.

00:21:26.310 --> 00:21:28.850
And then they can
authorize your app

00:21:28.850 --> 00:21:33.020
to use Google services like
Gmail on behalf of them

00:21:33.020 --> 00:21:34.130
in a secure manner.

00:21:40.060 --> 00:21:42.750
Google uses the
OAuth 2.0 protocols.

00:21:42.750 --> 00:21:44.680
So the way this works
is your application

00:21:44.680 --> 00:21:48.055
asks for user consent, like
you just saw, and only then it

00:21:48.055 --> 00:21:50.090
can access user data.

00:21:50.090 --> 00:21:55.030
So the key point here is that
your application with OS 2

00:21:55.030 --> 00:21:57.550
does never ask for the
user's credentials.

00:21:57.550 --> 00:22:00.700
It only gets consent to
then access user data

00:22:00.700 --> 00:22:01.810
on behalf of the user.

00:22:01.810 --> 00:22:06.770
So, remember, OAuth 2.0, it's
consent instead of credentials.

00:22:06.770 --> 00:22:10.720
Let's look at how the
protocol works in detail.

00:22:10.720 --> 00:22:14.680
This authorization flow is
also called three-legged OAuth.

00:22:14.680 --> 00:22:18.580
The entities are your JavaScript
front end, the Google Service,

00:22:18.580 --> 00:22:21.460
and the end user
that's consenting.

00:22:21.460 --> 00:22:24.580
So when you want to
access user data,

00:22:24.580 --> 00:22:27.400
you first send a request
to the Google Auth server,

00:22:27.400 --> 00:22:30.340
and then the end user has
to log in and consent.

00:22:30.340 --> 00:22:32.290
If they did that
successfully, then you

00:22:32.290 --> 00:22:34.060
get a token from
the Auth server,

00:22:34.060 --> 00:22:37.030
and now you can use this
token for all your requests

00:22:37.030 --> 00:22:40.420
to the Google APIs, like
Gmail, to send emails

00:22:40.420 --> 00:22:44.200
on behalf of that end user.

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:47.170
Luckily, the client
libraries handle this for us,

00:22:47.170 --> 00:22:49.840
so we don't have to worry
about the implementation

00:22:49.840 --> 00:22:53.190
details of three-legged OAuth.

00:22:53.190 --> 00:22:54.930
One thing that you
have to specify

00:22:54.930 --> 00:22:58.120
when you initialize the
clients to Google API's client

00:22:58.120 --> 00:22:59.100
is the scopes.

00:22:59.100 --> 00:23:01.440
Anu already mentioned
them earlier for Sheets.

00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:04.290
So now we're using
the Gmail.send scope.

00:23:04.290 --> 00:23:07.380
So that means when you
request the Gmail.send scope,

00:23:07.380 --> 00:23:10.170
when the user goes through
the Google assignment,

00:23:10.170 --> 00:23:12.360
then it actually says,
do you allow this app

00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:14.740
to send emails on your behalf?

00:23:14.740 --> 00:23:16.950
And if users approve
that, the token

00:23:16.950 --> 00:23:18.690
that we get back
from the Auth server

00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:20.700
is only good for this scope.

00:23:20.700 --> 00:23:22.450
If I take this token
now and I'm like,

00:23:22.450 --> 00:23:23.910
oh, I'm going to
touch your Sheets,

00:23:23.910 --> 00:23:28.470
then I get a 403, because that's
not what the user consented to.

00:23:28.470 --> 00:23:31.020
Obviously, you always
want to keep the scope as

00:23:31.020 --> 00:23:32.500
restrictive as possible.

00:23:32.500 --> 00:23:34.080
There's lots of
different scopes.

00:23:34.080 --> 00:23:35.790
Definitely have a
look at them if you're

00:23:35.790 --> 00:23:37.465
working with the Google APIs.

00:23:37.465 --> 00:23:41.170
I want to quickly point out
this one scope, drive.file.

00:23:41.170 --> 00:23:42.780
This is really nice,
because instead

00:23:42.780 --> 00:23:46.320
of granting access to all
your Sheets, for example,

00:23:46.320 --> 00:23:48.510
you're only granting
permission to the app

00:23:48.510 --> 00:23:53.130
to touch the Sheets that the
app itself created, or maybe

00:23:53.130 --> 00:23:55.050
explicitly was given permission.

00:23:55.050 --> 00:23:56.730
So this is a good
one, because users

00:23:56.730 --> 00:23:58.350
don't have to worry
that your app can

00:23:58.350 --> 00:24:01.830
read all their documents.

00:24:01.830 --> 00:24:04.440
All right, so on the front
end, we have a login button,

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:06.450
and then this consent
screen pops up,

00:24:06.450 --> 00:24:09.060
and the end user has to click
so that you can access--

00:24:09.060 --> 00:24:11.820
that you can send emails
on behalf of the users.

00:24:11.820 --> 00:24:15.030
How are we handling
Auth in our back end?

00:24:15.030 --> 00:24:17.730
We decided that our back
end--so the part where the Cloud

00:24:17.730 --> 00:24:20.100
Function talks to
the Google Sheet--

00:24:20.100 --> 00:24:22.900
will not have any
end users involved.

00:24:22.900 --> 00:24:27.430
Instead, we have that the sheet
belongs to the application.

00:24:27.430 --> 00:24:31.170
So our back end is not accessing
a new Sheet or my Sheet.

00:24:31.170 --> 00:24:33.450
It's accessing a Sheet
that actually belongs

00:24:33.450 --> 00:24:35.670
to this application,
and we're doing

00:24:35.670 --> 00:24:39.000
server-to-server
authorization here.

00:24:39.000 --> 00:24:42.720
Heads up, though-- if you're
working with the G Suite API,

00:24:42.720 --> 00:24:47.190
best practice is that you should
work with user credentials

00:24:47.190 --> 00:24:48.180
and three-legged OAuth.

00:24:48.180 --> 00:24:50.010
Usually, you don't
want the documents

00:24:50.010 --> 00:24:51.810
to belong to the application.

00:24:54.720 --> 00:24:59.270
So in the back end, we
have two-legged OAuth.

00:24:59.270 --> 00:25:00.500
We have two entities.

00:25:00.500 --> 00:25:03.340
We have the back end of the
application and the Google

00:25:03.340 --> 00:25:03.980
servers.

00:25:03.980 --> 00:25:07.600
There is no end
user involved here.

00:25:07.600 --> 00:25:10.210
And server-to-server
authentication

00:25:10.210 --> 00:25:15.940
uses JSON Web Tokens,
JWTs, to request a token

00:25:15.940 --> 00:25:17.360
from the Google Auth server.

00:25:17.360 --> 00:25:20.500
And then it can use that token
to access to Google APIs,

00:25:20.500 --> 00:25:22.750
but this time on behalf
of the application,

00:25:22.750 --> 00:25:25.360
not on behalf of somebody else.

00:25:25.360 --> 00:25:27.550
Again, here, use the
client libraries.

00:25:27.550 --> 00:25:29.590
They wrap this workflow
for you, so you

00:25:29.590 --> 00:25:32.590
don't have to create and
sign your own JSON Web Token.

00:25:35.720 --> 00:25:37.880
Comparing three-legged
and two-legged OAuth--

00:25:37.880 --> 00:25:42.590
so three-legged OAuth is always
on behalf of an end user,

00:25:42.590 --> 00:25:45.470
and it requires consent
from this end user.

00:25:45.470 --> 00:25:49.320
And for G Suite APIs,
the best practice

00:25:49.320 --> 00:25:51.110
is to use three-legged OAuth.

00:25:51.110 --> 00:25:53.210
Two-legged OAuth,
on the other hand,

00:25:53.210 --> 00:25:55.910
is server-to-server
authorization,

00:25:55.910 --> 00:25:59.330
and the requests are made on
behalf of the application.

00:25:59.330 --> 00:26:02.330
And the resources belong
to the application, not

00:26:02.330 --> 00:26:04.570
your end users.

00:26:04.570 --> 00:26:07.855
And under the hood, two-legged
OAuth is using JSON Web Tokens.

00:26:10.217 --> 00:26:12.550
FRANZISKA HINKELMANN: Thank
you for that really detailed

00:26:12.550 --> 00:26:15.010
comparison about the
different types of Auth.

00:26:15.010 --> 00:26:17.740
So like Franzie
said, this is all

00:26:17.740 --> 00:26:19.600
handled for you by
our client libraries

00:26:19.600 --> 00:26:21.460
if you choose to use them.

00:26:21.460 --> 00:26:23.890
If you look at the quick
starts in our documentation,

00:26:23.890 --> 00:26:25.570
they actually show
you how to get set up

00:26:25.570 --> 00:26:27.562
with this in just a few steps.

00:26:27.562 --> 00:26:29.020
It's really important
to use OAuth,

00:26:29.020 --> 00:26:31.870
because now you're
working with tokens,

00:26:31.870 --> 00:26:33.700
not people's actual passwords.

00:26:33.700 --> 00:26:36.130
We don't want people
storing them, especially

00:26:36.130 --> 00:26:38.480
not in plain text.

